I say next that it is also the least number they measure.

If not, then {A} and {B} measure some number {D} less than {C}

Let there be as many units in {E} as the times that {A} measures {D}, and as many units in {F} as the times that {B} measures {D}.

Then {A} multiplied by {E} makes {D}, and {B} multiplied by {F} makes {D}. Therefore the product of {A} and {E} equals the product of {B} and {F}. Therefore {A} is to {B} as {F} is to {E}. [!just VII.Def.15; VII.19]

But {A} and {B} are relatively prime, primes are also least, and the least measure the numbers which have the same ratio the same number of times, the greater the greater and the less the less, therefore {B} measures {E} as the consequent the consequent. [!just VII.21; VII.20]

And, since {A} multiplied by {B} and by {E} makes {C} and {D}, therefore {B} is to {E} as {C} is to {D}. But {B} measures {E}, therefore {C} also measures {D}, the greater the less, which is impossible. [!just VII.17]

Therefore {A} and {B} do not measure any number less than {C}. Therefore {C} is the least that is measured by {A} and {B}.

<hr width="40%">

Next, let {A:canvas_1} and {B:canvas_1} not be relatively prime. Take {F:canvas_1} and {E:canvas_1}, the least numbers of those which have the same ratio with {A:canvas_1} and {B:canvas_1}. Therefore the product of {A:canvas_1} and {E:canvas_1} equals the product of {B:canvas_1} and {F:canvas_1}. [!just VII.33; VII.19]

